# ============================================================
# Git Attributes for DNSCrypt Smart Filter
# Version: v1.2.0 (Global Edition)
# Author: gasciljh
# Repository: https://github.com/gasciljh/dnscrypt-proxy-webui
# https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes
# ============================================================
# Purpose:
#   Applies Git rules automatically for each file type.
#
# Scope:
#   Rules are defined only for file types that actually exist
#   in this repository. No speculative or defensive patterns.
#
# v1.1.0 notes (kept):
#   • `merge=union` is used ONLY for `CHANGELOG.md` and
#     `VERSION`. It is deliberately NOT used for files with
#     ordered content (SECURITY.md, docs/adr/README.md, etc.).
#   • The `export-ignore` list was reviewed — it excludes
#     development-only files from `git archive` and release
#     tarballs.
#
# ============================================================
# v1.2.0 changes:
#   • Version bumped from v1.1.0 to v1.2.0.
#
#   • 🔧 FIX (this revision): Removed the `-diff` attribute from
#     `proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version`.
#
#     Background:
#       `-diff` tells Git to treat a file as binary for the
#       purposes of `git diff` — it skips the text-based diff
#       algorithm and shows "Binary files ... differ" instead.
#
#     Why `-diff` was wrong here:
#       `proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version` is a single-line text
#       file (e.g. "2.1.18"). When the upstream DNS engine
#       version is bumped, the change is exactly one line:
#         -2.1.18
#         +2.1.19
#       Suppressing this diff with `-diff` HIDES the change
#       from every `git log -p`, `git show`, and PR review view.
#       That is the opposite of what we want for a file that is
#       the Single Source of Truth for the DNS engine version.
#
#     Why the ORIGINAL intent (never CRLF-normalize) does not
#     require `-diff`:
#       The `text eol=lf` attributes already handle line-ending
#       normalization. `-diff` only affects the diff display,
#       not the stored line endings.
#
#     The v1.1.0 change note for this file claimed `-diff` was
#     added as a preventive measure against binary treatment.
#     That was based on a misunderstanding: `-diff` does not
#     prevent binary treatment; it CAUSES it. The correct
#     attributes are `text eol=lf` alone.
#
#   • The `merge=union` list was re-verified:
#       - CHANGELOG.md  → still union (append-only)
#       - VERSION       → still union (single-line, but
#                         conflicts here are rare and
#                         resolvable by hand)
#     No new file was added to this list. In particular:
#       - docs/BACKUP.md      → NOT union (numbered sections)
#       - docs/EMERGENCY.md   → NOT union (numbered scenarios)
#       - docs/UPGRADE.md     → NOT union (versioned §3.X)
#     These files have ordered content and a union merge would
#     produce duplicated or interleaved sections.
#
#   • The `export-ignore` list was re-verified. The v1.2.0
#     release did NOT need any new export-ignore entry:
#       - docs/ already covers BACKUP.md, EMERGENCY.md, and
#         UPGRADE.md
#       - scripts/ already covers release-patch.sh
#       - .github/ already covers upgrade-test.yml and the
#         release PR template
#
#   • Added a "Data preservation (v1.2.0)" note in the
#     export-ignore section: the runtime backup directory
#     (/sdcard/dnscrypt-webui-backup/) lives OUTSIDE the
#     repository, so no export-ignore rule is needed for it.
#
#   • Global English edition: all comments are English-only.
#     No Arabic strings exist in this file.
#
# Cross-references (v1.2.0):
#   • docs/BACKUP.md      — backup system reference
#   • docs/EMERGENCY.md   — emergency recovery guide
#   • docs/UPGRADE.md §3.1 — v1.1.0 → v1.2.0 upgrade path
#   • Makefile            — `make package` creates dist/,
#                           `make check-docs` verifies docs
# ============================================================

# ============================================================
# Default: normalize line endings to LF on commit
# ============================================================
# Applies to every file unless a more specific rule below
# overrides it. LF is enforced because the module ships to
# Android (Linux) and is developed on Linux / macOS / WSL.
# ============================================================
* text=auto eol=lf

# ============================================================
# Shell scripts: LF only
# ============================================================
# Applies to both proxy/*.sh and scripts/*.sh. Explicit list
# is kept for the module-root shell scripts that get extracted
# to /data/adb/modules/ (where CRLF would break execution).
#
# v1.2.0 note: scripts/release-patch.sh and scripts/release.sh
# are covered by `*.sh`. No new explicit entry was needed.
# ============================================================
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.bash        text eol=lf
action.sh     text eol=lf
customize.sh  text eol=lf
functions.sh  text eol=lf
post-fs-data.sh text eol=lf
service.sh    text eol=lf
status.sh     text eol=lf
uninstall.sh  text eol=lf
watchdog.sh   text eol=lf

# ============================================================
# Go: LF only
# ============================================================
# Go 1.22 is the current toolchain (see proxy/go.mod). Go source
# files use tabs for indentation (enforced by gofmt), but line
# endings must be LF on every platform.
# ============================================================
*.go          text eol=lf

# ============================================================
# Web: LF only
# ============================================================
*.html        text eol=lf
*.css         text eol=lf
*.js          text eol=lf
*.json        text eol=lf
*.svg         text eol=lf

# ============================================================
# Config: LF only
# ============================================================
*.toml        text eol=lf
*.conf        text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.md          text eol=lf

# ============================================================
# module.prop: LF only
# ============================================================
# The Magisk parser is sensitive to line endings. A CRLF here
# would cause `version=` and `versionCode=` to be read with a
# trailing \r, breaking update detection.
# ============================================================
module.prop   text eol=lf

# ============================================================
# VERSION: LF only, single line
# ============================================================
# Single-line file. Read by release.sh, build.sh, Makefile,
# and release.yml. Any trailing whitespace or CR would break
# version parsing.
# ============================================================
VERSION       text eol=lf

# ============================================================
# proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version: LF only, single line
# ============================================================
# The Single Source of Truth for the upstream dnscrypt-proxy
# version (e.g. "2.1.18"). Read by:
#   • scripts/fetch_dns_binaries.sh
#   • scripts/package_module.sh
#   • proxy/build.sh (indirectly, via release.yml)
#
# This file is NOT the module version (that is VERSION).
# It is updated manually when the upstream DNSCrypt project
# ships a new release. See docs/DNS_BINARIES.md §10.
#
# Any whitespace or CRLF would break the version check regex
# (`^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$`) in fetch_dns_binaries.sh.
#
# 🔧 v1.2.0 FIX: The `-diff` attribute was REMOVED from this
#    rule. It is a single-line text file; `-diff` would hide
#    the version bump from `git log -p`, `git show`, and PR
#    reviews. The `text eol=lf` attributes are sufficient to
#    guarantee correct line endings and text treatment.
#
#    See the file header ("v1.2.0 changes" → 🔧 FIX) for the
#    full rationale.
# ============================================================
proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version  text eol=lf

# ============================================================
# Go mod: LF only, no formatting
# ============================================================
# go.mod and go.sum are formatted by the Go toolchain. Do not
# run formatters on them.
#
# v1.2.0 note: proxy/go.mod is unchanged from v1.1.0. No new
# imports were added by the data-preservation release.
# ============================================================
go.mod        text eol=lf
go.sum        text eol=lf

# ============================================================
# Makefile: LF only (recipes require tabs)
# ============================================================
# The `SHELL` variable and recipe lines use tabs. Git must not
# convert them. LF-only is mandatory.
#
# v1.2.0 note: the Makefile gained four new targets
# (check-backup, check-all, check-docs, diagnose-help). All
# use tabs in their recipes. No rule change was needed.
# ============================================================
Makefile      text eol=lf

# ============================================================
# Binary files (no conversion)
# ============================================================
# Any file listed below is treated as binary by Git. Line
# ending conversion, diff, and merge are disabled for them.
# ============================================================
*.png         binary
*.jpg         binary
*.jpeg        binary
*.gif         binary
*.ico         binary
*.pdf         binary
*.zip         binary
*.gz          binary
*.tar         binary
*.tar.gz      binary
*.7z          binary

# ============================================================
# Built binaries (should not be committed)
# ============================================================
# Safety net — these patterns ensure that even if a developer
# accidentally stages a binary, Git treats it as binary (no
# diff, no CRLF conversion, no merge).
# ============================================================
dnscrypt-webui*  binary
dnscrypt-proxy   binary

# ============================================================
# Runtime artifacts (proxy/run/)
# ============================================================
# Files created at runtime by main.go. Never committed, but
# if they exist locally, Git treats them as binary.
#
# Data preservation (v1.2.0):
#   The persistent backup directory
#   (/sdcard/dnscrypt-webui-backup/) lives OUTSIDE the
#   repository. No rule is needed for it here.
# ============================================================
proxy/run/*.status         binary
proxy/run/*.pid            binary
proxy/run/*.tmp            binary
proxy/run/*.tmp_*          binary
proxy/run/.bootstrap_cache binary

# ============================================================
# Atomic-write temp files
# ============================================================
# main.go uses atomicWriteFile / atomicWriteStream, which create
# short-lived temp files named `<target>.tmp_<random>`.
# Treating them as binary avoids meaningless diffs.
# ============================================================
*.tmp_*                    binary

# ============================================================
# Diff behavior
# ============================================================
# Improves `git diff` output for common file types.
#
# 🔧 v1.2.0 FIX: `proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version` is NOT listed
# here with `-diff`. A single-line text file must be diffable
# so that version bumps are visible in `git log -p`.
# ============================================================
*.md          diff=markdown
*.go          diff=golang
*.sh          diff=shell

go.sum        -diff
checksums.txt -diff

# ============================================================
# Linguist (GitHub language detection)
# ============================================================
# Controls the language bar on the repository homepage.
#   • docs/**          → documentation (not counted as code)
#   • *.md, *.txt      → documentation
#   • *.sh             → Shell
#   • *.html           → HTML
#   • web/sw.js        → JavaScript
#   • .github/**       → not counted at all
#
# docs/adr/*.md is automatically covered by `docs/**` and
# `*.md`. No explicit rule needed.
#
# v1.2.0 note: docs/BACKUP.md, docs/EMERGENCY.md, and
# docs/UPGRADE.md are covered by `docs/**` automatically.
# ============================================================
docs/**       linguist-documentation
*.md          linguist-documentation
*.txt         linguist-documentation

*.sh          linguist-language=Shell
*.html        linguist-language=HTML
web/sw.js     linguist-language=JavaScript
.github/**    linguist-detectable=false

# ============================================================
# Merge Strategy — Binary files
# ============================================================
# Binary files cannot be merged with the default strategy.
# Git falls back to "ours" and reports a conflict. This is
# the correct behavior — the developer must regenerate the
# file instead of merging bytes.
# ============================================================
*.zip         merge=binary
*.gz          merge=binary
*.png         merge=binary
*.svg         merge=binary

proxy/run/*.status         merge=binary
proxy/run/.bootstrap_cache merge=binary

# ============================================================
# Merge Strategy — Workflow files
# ============================================================
# ⚠️ Use `merge=union` ONLY for files with append-only
#    semantics. Do NOT apply it to ordered files.
#
# CHANGELOG.md:
#   Append-only. When two branches add entries to different
#   sections, both must be preserved. `union` concatenates
#   them without conflict.
#
# VERSION:
#   Single-line file. Two concurrent version bumps are rare,
#   but `union` prevents a conflict if they happen (the
#   result is a two-line VERSION, which a human resolves).
#
# ⚠️ Deliberately NOT union-merged:
#   • SECURITY.md — Audit Corrections are ordered.
#   • docs/SECURITY.md — same reason.
#   • docs/adr/README.md — ADR index is ordered by number.
#   • docs/BRANCHING.md — structured sections.
#   • docs/BACKUP.md — v1.2.0, numbered sections (§1–§13).
#   • docs/EMERGENCY.md — v1.2.0, numbered scenarios (§1–§12).
#   • docs/UPGRADE.md — v1.2.0, versioned sections (§3.0, §3.1).
# ============================================================
CHANGELOG.md  -diff merge=union

VERSION       merge=union

# ============================================================
# Export-ignore (not included in git archive)
# ============================================================
# Files listed below are excluded from `git archive` and from
# the release tarballs generated by GitHub (which uses
# `git archive` under the hood).
#
# ⚠️ Do NOT export-ignore anything under proxy/ or web/ —
#    those directories ship with the module.
#
# The release pipeline uses scripts/package_module.sh, which
# reads from the working tree (not from `git archive`), so
# this list only affects the GitHub-generated source tarball
# (the "Source code (zip)" link on the Releases page).
#
# Data preservation (v1.2.0):
#   The runtime backup directory
#   (/sdcard/dnscrypt-webui-backup/) is NOT part of the
#   repository. It never appears in `git archive` and does not
#   need an export-ignore rule.
#
#   The runtime file proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version is INCLUDED
#   intentionally — it is the Single Source of Truth for the
#   DNS engine version and is needed by scripts that consume
#   the tarball.
# ============================================================
.github/       export-ignore
.gitignore     export-ignore
.gitattributes export-ignore
.editorconfig  export-ignore
.pre-commit-config.yaml export-ignore
.secrets.baseline export-ignore
docs/          export-ignore
scripts/       export-ignore
.devcontainer/ export-ignore
Makefile       export-ignore
build/         export-ignore
dist/          export-ignore
*.md           export-ignore
proxy/build.sh      export-ignore

proxy/run/     export-ignore